WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.977
At the end of a busy day, the fruit shop keeps a few facts about each order.

2
00:00:07.977 --> 00:00:10.944
Which varieties were chosen: Gala and Fuji.

3
00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:12.669
The weight of each piece.

4
00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:14.877
And the total weight: 330 grams.

5
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.352
These facts are different kinds of thing.

6
00:00:17.352 --> 00:00:20.474
A list of names, a list of numbers, and a single number.

7
00:00:20.474 --> 00:00:22.425
So where do you keep them together?

8
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.901
The familiar answer is one map, from a name to a value.

9
00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:29.302
To that map, every value is just Any.

10
00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:30.405
And remember Any?

11
00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:34.816
Back in the first lesson, we said its honest name would be whatever.

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.371
So every read needs a cast, a trust me to the compiler.

13
00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.720
And names can collide.

14
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.111
Two helpers both pick the name weights.

15
00:00:42.111 --> 00:00:44.808
One stores the weight of each piece, a list.

16
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:47.505
The other stores the total, a single number.

17
00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:53.941
The second write lands in the same slot, and the first helper reads back a number where it wanted a list.

18
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:57.750
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

19
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.066
I already have names.

20
00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:02.012
Why would a key need anything more than a name?

21
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:06.582
Remember the card index from the lesson on type tokens?

22
00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:08.714
There, the type was the whole key.

23
00:01:08.714 --> 00:01:11.661
This time, give each card a tab with two lines.

24
00:01:11.661 --> 00:01:13.668
The top line is a name: weights.

25
00:01:13.668 --> 00:01:18.057
The bottom line is the type of what goes on the card: a list of grams.

26
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:21.148
And at the drawer stands our clerk.

27
00:01:21.148 --> 00:01:24.122
To file a card, you show the tab and the value.

28
00:01:24.122 --> 00:01:26.021
The clerk reads the type line.

29
00:01:26.021 --> 00:01:28.868
A list of names under the varieties tab fits.

30
00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:32.855
The number 42 under the same tab doesn't, and it's turned away.

31
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:33.877
Let's map it.

32
00:01:33.877 --> 00:01:35.227
The tab is a Key&lt;T&gt;.

33
00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:37.387
The name line is the key's name.

34
00:01:37.387 --> 00:01:41.774
The type line is a KType, made by typeOf when you create the key.

35
00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:47.241
The drawer is the container, and the clerk is the compiler, checking every write.

36
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.467
Here it is in code.

37
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.555
Key is a data class with a name and a KType.

38
00:01:52.555 --> 00:01:57.820
The key function is inline, with a reified T, so typeOf sees the real type.

39
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.698
VARIETIES is a key for a list of strings.

40
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:02.593
set takes a Key&lt;T&gt; and a T.

41
00:02:02.593 --> 00:02:06.243
And get takes a Key&lt;T&gt;, and hands back a T, or null.

42
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:14.575
Java has no typeOf, so its key gets the full type from a super type token, the anonymous subclass trick from the same lesson.

43
00:02:14.575 --> 00:02:16.013
The shape is the same.

44
00:02:16.933 --> 00:02:22.282
The lesson's demo files the varieties and the total, then reads them back.

45
00:02:22.282 --> 00:02:24.884
It prints: varieties, Gala and Fuji.

46
00:02:24.884 --> 00:02:26.113
Total, 330 grams.

47
00:02:26.113 --> 00:02:27.558
Now notice the read.

48
00:02:27.558 --> 00:02:31.462
No cast at the call site, and no type argument either.

49
00:02:31.462 --> 00:02:33.485
The key's T does the typing.

50
00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:37.016
And the wrong write, 42 under the varieties key?

51
00:02:37.016 --> 00:02:43.086
Kotlin says: argument type mismatch, actual type is Int, but List&lt;String&gt; was expected.

52
00:02:43.086 --> 00:02:48.599
And Java says: method set in class Attributes cannot be applied to given types.

53
00:02:49.467 --> 00:02:52.032
And our two helpers who both chose weights?

54
00:02:52.032 --> 00:02:58.238
A key named weights for a list of ints, and a key named weights for a single Int, have different KTypes.

55
00:02:58.238 --> 00:03:02.116
So they are different keys, and the two values land in two slots.

56
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.168
One cast is left, inside get, and it's unchecked.

57
00:03:06.168 --> 00:03:11.211
It's safe because the only way in is set, and set only takes a T for a Key&lt;T&gt;.

58
00:03:11.211 --> 00:03:13.473
That's called safe by construction.

59
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.747
Now let's be fair to a tempting idea.

60
00:03:16.747 --> 00:03:18.333
A key never takes a T in.

61
00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:19.982
It has no T member at all.

62
00:03:19.982 --> 00:03:24.042
So, by the habit this track has built, it looks like a producer.

63
00:03:24.042 --> 00:03:27.340
Why not declare it out T, and make it more flexible?

64
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:31.323
The lesson's demo tries exactly that, with a LeakyKey.

65
00:03:31.323 --> 00:03:34.201
It writes 42 under a leaky varieties key.

66
00:03:34.201 --> 00:03:35.815
Does that line compile?

67
00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:37.921
And if something fails, where?

68
00:03:37.921 --> 00:03:39.044
Pause and guess.

69
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:43.752
The write compiles.

70
00:03:43.752 --> 00:03:48.600
With out T, a key for a list of strings is also a key for Any.

71
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.884
So T is inferred as Any, and 42 is an Any.

72
00:03:51.884 --> 00:04:01.344
The failure comes later, at the read: ClassCastException, class java.lang.Integer cannot be cast to class java.util.List.

73
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:08.203
In the picture, out T lets a tab that says list of strings pose as a tab that says anything.

74
00:04:08.203 --> 00:04:11.752
The clerk reads anything, and waves the 42 straight in.

75
00:04:11.933 --> 00:04:15.095
So a key with out T is a wonderfully flexible key.

76
00:04:15.095 --> 00:04:19.141
It opens every lock, including the ones you wanted to stay shut.

77
00:04:19.141 --> 00:04:25.969
The point is this: a key decides what may be written, so it can't be a producer, however few members it has.

78
00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:27.759
Now the names.

79
00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:31.774
A key that carries the type of its value is called a typed key.

80
00:04:31.774 --> 00:04:37.511
And keeping it invariant, neither out nor in, is the last variance decision of this track.

81
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.386
Two guardrails.

82
00:04:39.386 --> 00:04:42.804
First, this is a good place to stop adding generics.

83
00:04:42.804 --> 00:04:50.166
If a design needs much more than this, a sealed class and a when are usually easier for the next person to read.

84
00:04:50.166 --> 00:04:51.678
Second, know the limit.

85
00:04:51.678 --> 00:04:54.241
These designs check types, not meaning.

86
00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.085
The compiler will happily file a weight of minus 50 grams under exactly the right key.

87
00:05:00.085 --> 00:05:02.861
Perfectly typed, and perfectly impossible.

88
00:05:03.733 --> 00:05:06.190
So, why would a key need more than a name?

89
00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.754
Because the name says where a value goes, and the type says what may go there.

90
00:05:10.754 --> 00:05:14.147
Let the key carry its value's type, so reads need no cast.

91
00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:17.072
And keep the key invariant, because it writes too.

92
00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:18.131
Quick check.

93
00:05:18.131 --> 00:05:23.680
With Key declared out T, which line fails: the write of 42, or the next read?

94
00:05:26.933 --> 00:05:27.569
The read.

95
00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:30.605
The write compiles, with T inferred as Any.

96
00:05:30.605 --> 00:05:33.076
The read throws ClassCastException.
